Go libraries used by server-hardening, and why.

Direct dependencies
-------------------
github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea
    The terminal loop of the full-screen interface: raw keyboard input, the
    alternate screen, resize events, and drawing the frame. It is used only in
    internal/app. The screens themselves (internal/screen) never touch the
    terminal, so they are tested without one.

github.com/charmbracelet/x/ansi
    Measures the width of text in terminal cells (emoji take two) and cuts
    text to a width. Used by internal/screen to keep every line aligned.

github.com/charmbracelet/huh
    The simple one-question prompts of --plain, and the fallback for a
    terminal that is not interactive or not UTF-8. It is used only in
    internal/tui.

Everything else (controls, engine, records) uses the Go standard library and
does not import any of them, so the controls can be tested without a terminal.

Indirect dependencies
---------------------
These libraries pull in the other Charm libraries (bubbles, lipgloss) and a
few terminal helpers. The full, exact list with versions is in go.sum, and
`go list -m all` prints it. `go mod verify` checks that the downloaded modules
match go.sum.

Not used
--------
No library is used to run commands, edit files, talk to systemd or parse
configuration. Controls call the system's own tools (apt-get, systemctl, sshd,
nft, ...) through one small runner, internal/execx, so each action can be read
in the log exactly as it ran.

Updating
--------
Dependencies are pinned by go.mod and go.sum. Update them on purpose
(`go get -u ./... && go mod tidy`), read the changes, and run the tests.
